  "metadata": {
    "alignment": "unspecified",
    "description": "Single-DGX-Spark Qwen3.8 Flash Next on Ash Hart's TensorFold engine v0.4.0 (exact decoding, custom CUDA kernels JIT-compiled on first start, stock upstream source) with turboderp's EXL3 3.05 bits-per-weight pack (branch 3.05bpw_h5_ng5; 85 GB including a 32.6 GB n-gram table that stays memory-mapped from the checkpoint; MTP layer included). TensorFold marks its EXL3 path experimental and runs it on one GPU only (upstream refuses --tp 2 and --ple-on-ssd on EXL3 packs); the platform harness has no distributed support anyway. Serves one request at a time by default (upstream CUDA default; the concurrency setting enables shared rounds) at the 262,144-token window with a bf16 KV cache. Text only: stock TensorFold has no image or video path for Flash Next. Upstream measured on one DGX Spark: 81 tok/s code sampled and 59 tok/s chat sampled (MLX 4-bit checkpoint: 77 and 63), prefill at about 0.4x the MLX checkpoint, 52 GB of GPU memory for the weights and 57.6 GiB allocated after loading at 262,144 tokens (upstream claims, unmeasured here); the memory envelope below adds the mapped n-gram table to that figure. Only the 3.05bpw_h5_ng5 branch is measured upstream. The model weights are under the Qwen Community License 1.0 (attribution above 100M MAU or US$20M monthly revenue; separate licence for Model-as-a-Service or AI Work Assistant businesses); TensorFold is MIT.",
    "tags": [
      "engine",
      "kernel-jit",
      "exl3",
      "experimental",
      "mtp",
      "qwen3.8",
      "reasoning",
      "spark",
      "tensorfold"
    ],
    "title": "Qwen3.8 Flash Next EXL3 3.05bpw TensorFold single Spark"
  },
